00:34:12.614 --> 00:34:15.781
- It's a nice try, really is.

00:34:15.781 --> 00:34:18.833
But it's (beep), Rashid.

00:34:18.833 --> 00:34:21.782
Come on, is that the best you got that?

00:34:21.782 --> 00:34:23.193
Now come on, I'm listening.

00:34:23.193 --> 00:34:24.651
Just tell me, tell me.

00:34:24.651 --> 00:34:27.810
Tell me about this imminent attack.

00:34:27.810 --> 00:34:29.054
- What, what?

00:34:29.054 --> 00:34:29.887
What attack?

00:34:29.887 --> 00:34:30.991
I don't know what you're talking about.

00:34:30.991 --> 00:34:32.553
- What am I talking about?

00:34:32.553 --> 00:34:33.636
I don't know, huh?

00:34:33.636 --> 00:34:34.469
Huh?

00:34:34.469 --> 00:34:35.334
I don't know what you're talking about.

00:34:35.334 --> 00:34:36.326
- [Rashid] I'm telling you.

00:34:36.326 --> 00:34:37.159
- No, you don't know?

00:34:37.159 --> 00:34:37.992
- [Rashid] I don't know.

00:34:37.992 --> 00:34:38.825
I don't know.

00:34:38.825 --> 00:34:40.994
- June 11th, Rashid.

00:34:40.994 --> 00:34:42.442
You spoke of an imminent attack.

00:34:42.442 --> 00:34:44.914
- Oh, yes, that was my friend.

00:34:44.914 --> 00:34:47.173
Listen, his dog bit him.

00:34:47.173 --> 00:34:49.421
And I told him if you do not train them,

00:34:49.421 --> 00:34:52.434
another terrier attack is imminent.

00:34:52.434 --> 00:34:55.043
(thudding)
(grunting)

00:34:55.043 --> 00:34:56.495
(coughing)

00:34:56.495 --> 00:34:58.944
- [Wilson] See, you lied,
and you see what happens?

00:34:58.944 --> 00:35:00.586
- I'm not lying to you.

00:35:00.586 --> 00:35:01.419
- Come on, Rashid.

00:35:01.419 --> 00:35:02.773
I like you.

00:35:02.773 --> 00:35:04.952
Let's be friends, huh?

00:35:04.952 --> 00:35:07.621
Now you still haven't
explained to me why we found

00:35:07.621 --> 00:35:09.992
on the search engine of your computer

00:35:09.992 --> 00:35:13.351
the words "Target Washington" 49 times?

00:35:13.351 --> 00:35:16.562
- That is because I was
in DC for a dog show

00:35:16.562 --> 00:35:18.144
and I had to buy a squeezy toy,

00:35:18.144 --> 00:35:19.802
a birthday card and some deodorant,

00:35:19.802 --> 00:35:22.181
all at an affordable price.
- All right.

00:35:22.181 --> 00:35:24.321
- You can only do that at Target.

00:35:24.321 --> 00:35:27.453
- Yeah, okay, I see how
you want to play this.

00:35:27.453 --> 00:35:28.664
Give me this.

00:35:28.664 --> 00:35:32.802
This is not a case of
mistaken identity, Rashid,

00:35:32.802 --> 00:35:34.562
and you know it.

00:35:34.562 --> 00:35:37.237
We've heard the conversations
between you and Jafar

00:35:37.237 --> 00:35:40.165
where you're discussing your
hatred of American ideals?

00:35:40.165 --> 00:35:41.144
- No, no, idols.

00:35:41.144 --> 00:35:43.795
American idols, I hate them.

00:35:43.795 --> 00:35:45.045
They performed "Dancing Queen."

00:35:45.045 --> 00:35:48.962
It was a desecration to
Abba, my favorite band.

00:35:50.786 --> 00:35:53.055
- We got you on tape referring to Jafar

00:35:53.055 --> 00:35:54.637
as one of the architects of--

00:35:54.637 --> 00:35:58.997
- [Rashid] 7/11, it was 7/11.

00:35:58.997 --> 00:36:02.080
Jafar, he designs convenience stores.

00:36:05.654 --> 00:36:07.815
- 7/11?

00:36:07.815 --> 00:36:09.466
Oh, (beep).

00:36:09.466 --> 00:36:14.247
- Wilson, 65 dogs just
attacked the Capitol Building.

00:36:14.247 --> 00:36:15.895
- Were they terriers?

00:36:15.895 --> 00:36:16.802
- How did you know?

00:36:16.802 --> 00:36:20.335
- (beep).
- [Rashid] Praise Abba.

00:36:20.335 --> 00:36:22.997
(tense music)

00:36:22.997 --> 00:36:26.292
- It is so good to see you, Keith.

00:36:26.292 --> 00:36:28.848
- Yeah, we usually have to
wait for a special occasion.

00:36:28.848 --> 00:36:30.430
- Well, actually I have a little surprise.

00:36:30.430 --> 00:36:31.597
I got engaged.

00:36:32.530 --> 00:36:37.113
(squealing)
(laughing)

00:36:38.242 --> 00:36:40.992
- Son.
(clapping)

00:36:43.791 --> 00:36:47.791
- Woohoo, yes, wow,
congratulations, little bro.

00:36:52.332 --> 00:36:54.665
Engaged, kind of a big deal.

00:36:56.289 --> 00:36:57.750
More for me. I guess.

00:36:57.750 --> 00:37:00.003
That's what that means.

00:37:00.003 --> 00:37:03.963
- Yes, well, she got a name?
- Stephanie.

00:37:03.963 --> 00:37:06.801
- Marriage is a wonderful journey.

00:37:06.801 --> 00:37:09.603
- 50% end in the divorce.

00:37:09.603 --> 00:37:12.436
Sorry, bro, them's just the facts.

00:37:13.432 --> 00:37:17.272
- I will, I will keep
that in mind, thank you.

00:37:17.272 --> 00:37:18.222
- You got a photo?

00:37:18.222 --> 00:37:21.403
- Oh yeah.
- AKA, does she exist?

00:37:21.403 --> 00:37:22.762
There you go.

00:37:22.762 --> 00:37:23.595
(whistling)

00:37:23.595 --> 00:37:25.845
(laughing)

00:37:28.303 --> 00:37:30.136
- Oh, she's beautiful.

00:37:32.597 --> 00:37:34.896
- Let me get eyes on this.

00:37:34.896 --> 00:37:37.313
Everybody else already ha ...

00:37:40.251 --> 00:37:43.001
Great lighting, airbrushed?
- No.

00:37:44.771 --> 00:37:46.949
- She kind of looks like that one model

00:37:46.949 --> 00:37:47.990
on "The Price Is Right."

00:37:47.990 --> 00:37:49.490
- Yes, that's her.

00:37:51.349 --> 00:37:52.851
- "The Price Is Right?"

00:37:52.851 --> 00:37:54.870
Clive, that's your show.

00:37:54.870 --> 00:37:56.342
- Shut up, mom.

00:37:56.342 --> 00:37:57.425
No, it's not.

00:38:00.262 --> 00:38:03.845
Oh, yeah, Stephanie,
people always underbid

00:38:04.963 --> 00:38:06.451
on her showcases.

00:38:06.451 --> 00:38:08.368
Not the best presenter.

00:38:10.353 --> 00:38:11.283
- Where's the wedding?

00:38:11.283 --> 00:38:13.390
- Oh, in Hawaii.

00:38:13.390 --> 00:38:15.890
- Ooh.
(claps)

00:38:18.033 --> 00:38:22.866
- Awesome, woohoo, (in foreign language).

00:38:26.083 --> 00:38:27.374
She ever been married before?

00:38:27.374 --> 00:38:29.539
- No.
- So she says.

00:38:29.539 --> 00:38:31.557
Ex-boyfriend, she got one?

00:38:31.557 --> 00:38:34.637
- Of course.
- Sloppy seconds, at least.

00:38:34.637 --> 00:38:35.554
Right, Dad?

00:38:38.525 --> 00:38:40.565
- Keith, she sounds wonderful.

00:38:40.565 --> 00:38:41.398
- [Keith] Thank you, Mon.

00:38:41.398 --> 00:38:42.648
- Shut up, Mom.

00:38:44.117 --> 00:38:46.283
Silence from you.

00:38:46.283 --> 00:38:48.616
You're cut off from talking.

00:38:51.295 --> 00:38:53.365
- You know what, Clive?

00:38:53.365 --> 00:38:54.757
I really don't appreciate the way

00:38:54.757 --> 00:38:57.007
that you're talking to mom.

00:38:58.480 --> 00:39:01.321
(clapping)
(laughing)

00:39:01.321 --> 00:39:04.654
Yes, yes, the prodigal son has returned.

00:39:07.354 --> 00:39:08.314
You know what?

00:39:08.314 --> 00:39:09.205
You want to go?

00:39:09.205 --> 00:39:12.565
Let's go, 'cause I've been
here for the last 15 years

00:39:12.565 --> 00:39:15.386
taking care of mom and dad
while you've been trollopsing

00:39:15.386 --> 00:39:17.434
all over the world and why?

00:39:17.434 --> 00:39:19.736
So you can come back here and criticize me

00:39:19.736 --> 00:39:21.354
for how I treat them?

00:39:21.354 --> 00:39:24.437
- No, I actually came here to ask you

00:39:25.525 --> 00:39:28.861
if you would do me the
honor of being my best man?

00:39:28.861 --> 00:39:31.444
(gentle music)

00:39:33.236 --> 00:39:34.864
(snorting)

00:39:34.864 --> 00:39:38.741
I know that we have had our differences,

00:39:38.741 --> 00:39:41.324
but you're my older brother.

00:39:41.324 --> 00:39:43.674
(snorting)
(gentle music)

00:39:43.674 --> 00:39:45.645
We'll pay for your room

00:39:45.645 --> 00:39:47.825
and we're also going to rent you a car

00:39:47.825 --> 00:39:50.742
and we'll pay to fly you out there.

00:39:51.894 --> 00:39:54.986
(sobbing)
(gentle music)

00:39:54.986 --> 00:39:56.735
- First class?

00:39:56.735 --> 00:39:58.527
- Well, we can't afford first class.

00:39:58.527 --> 00:40:00.957
- Well, (beep) you.

00:40:00.957 --> 00:40:02.457
You're dead to me.

00:40:03.471 --> 00:40:05.351
- [Mom] Clive.
- [Clive] Shut up, Mom.

00:40:05.351 --> 00:40:07.184
I'm eating in my room.

00:40:14.088 --> 00:40:17.372
- Well, that, that went
better than expected.

00:40:17.372 --> 00:40:19.122
- I think so.
- Yeah.

00:40:22.169 --> 00:40:24.336
(tapping)

00:40:26.481 --> 00:40:29.231
(buzzer buzzing)

00:40:34.301 --> 00:40:35.916
- Hey man, how you doing there?

00:40:35.916 --> 00:40:38.916
You guys got bow and arrows?
- Yeah.

00:40:40.871 --> 00:40:41.842
- Oh yeah, oh, that's it.

00:40:41.842 --> 00:40:42.675
Yeah, there it is.

00:40:42.675 --> 00:40:43.508
That'll do it.

00:40:43.508 --> 00:40:44.341
That's going to do it.

00:40:44.341 --> 00:40:46.758
Yup, where are the arrows at?

00:40:47.652 --> 00:40:49.509
Yeah, you guys got M-80s?

00:40:49.509 --> 00:40:51.077
- We don't carry fireworks.

00:40:51.077 --> 00:40:53.947
- Damn, you have a, you have
something else that's explosive

00:40:53.947 --> 00:40:57.387
that I could tape to the end of an arrow?

00:40:57.387 --> 00:41:00.638
- We have shotgun shells,
but I feel I have to ask,

00:41:00.638 --> 00:41:02.477
are you planning to attach those to arrows

00:41:02.477 --> 00:41:04.129
and use them as a weapon?

00:41:04.129 --> 00:41:06.245
- What, no, what, are you crazy?

00:41:06.245 --> 00:41:07.703
You think that would work though?

00:41:07.703 --> 00:41:10.437
What if I shot explosive
arrows through a window

00:41:10.437 --> 00:41:12.117
to break them open?

00:41:12.117 --> 00:41:14.566
- Sir, I feel like I have to
ask you what you're planning

00:41:14.566 --> 00:41:15.440
to use these for.

00:41:15.440 --> 00:41:16.689
- Oh, no, I'm not planning anything.

00:41:16.689 --> 00:41:17.948
I'm just Christmas shopping.

00:41:17.948 --> 00:41:18.781
I'm getting 'em.

00:41:18.781 --> 00:41:19.739
You got zip lines?
- Nope.

00:41:19.739 --> 00:41:22.119
- But when you got like
hooks and anchors and cables?

00:41:22.119 --> 00:41:23.519
- We've got some of those things,

00:41:23.519 --> 00:41:26.557
but I do not recommend
manufacturing your own zip line.

00:41:26.557 --> 00:41:29.226
- Oh, I'm not doing that, no.

00:41:29.226 --> 00:41:31.705
Let's say somebody was
manufacturing their own zip line.

00:41:31.705 --> 00:41:33.275
Do you think if they had
busted some windows open

00:41:33.275 --> 00:41:35.654
on a building somehow
then fired the zip line

00:41:35.654 --> 00:41:37.545
off a bow and arrow and it attached

00:41:37.545 --> 00:41:38.774
to the wall of said building,

00:41:38.774 --> 00:41:41.835
that they could just ride the
zip line into the building?

00:41:41.835 --> 00:41:43.384
- I have no idea, sir.

00:41:43.384 --> 00:41:46.664
Whatever you're planning on
doing, please don't do it.

00:41:46.664 --> 00:41:48.065
- I'm not doing anything.

00:41:48.065 --> 00:41:49.385
I'm just doing some Christmas shopping.

00:41:49.385 --> 00:41:50.476
I'm trying to get it out of the way.

00:41:50.476 --> 00:41:51.663
Bow and arrow's for my nephew.

00:41:51.663 --> 00:41:55.534
The zip line's for my grandmother,
et cetera and whatnot.

00:41:55.534 --> 00:41:56.367
You know what I'm saying?

00:41:56.367 --> 00:41:57.625
No big deal.

00:41:57.625 --> 00:41:58.585
Speaking of other things,

00:41:58.585 --> 00:42:03.585
do you guys have just the top
half of a knight in armor?

00:42:03.855 --> 00:42:06.124
- First of all, it's
called a suit of armor.

00:42:06.124 --> 00:42:06.957
- Yep, that's it.

00:42:06.957 --> 00:42:08.209
That's right, that's
what I'm meant to say.

00:42:08.209 --> 00:42:10.409
- [Uncle] We have one, but
it's sold as a complete set.

00:42:10.409 --> 00:42:12.180
- Yup, yup, yeah, well,
the person it's for,

00:42:12.180 --> 00:42:13.588
they only need the, they don't need the,

00:42:13.588 --> 00:42:14.421
they just need the top half.

00:42:14.421 --> 00:42:15.629
They don't need the legs.

00:42:15.629 --> 00:42:18.580
Well, what if somebody
was shooting at you?

00:42:18.580 --> 00:42:20.405
What were the chances you think
they try to shoot your legs?

00:42:20.405 --> 00:42:23.596
- I feel like I have to tell
you that a suit of armor

00:42:23.596 --> 00:42:26.435
will not deflect bullets.

00:42:26.435 --> 00:42:29.937
- I'll take my chances, with Christmas.

00:42:29.937 --> 00:42:31.537
Which brings me to my next question,

00:42:31.537 --> 00:42:33.897
do you guys have roller skates?

00:42:33.897 --> 00:42:36.141
- I think we have a pair around.

00:42:36.141 --> 00:42:36.974
- Let me ask you a question.

00:42:36.974 --> 00:42:38.417
How fast do you think you have
to be going on roller skates

00:42:38.417 --> 00:42:39.406
to get past a couple of armed guards

00:42:39.406 --> 00:42:42.616
and still be going fast enough
to smash through a safe door?

00:42:42.616 --> 00:42:44.006
- One of those big bank safes?

00:42:44.006 --> 00:42:46.589
- No, I don't know, yeah, yeah?

00:42:49.431 --> 00:42:50.505
Hey, you guys got one of those,

00:42:50.505 --> 00:42:53.809
those really big bags with a
dollar sign on the side of it?

00:42:53.809 --> 00:42:57.508
- Okay, you're going to shoot arrows

00:42:57.508 --> 00:42:59.722
with shotgun shells attached
to them to a bank window

00:42:59.722 --> 00:43:00.958
to blow out the glass.

00:43:00.958 --> 00:43:03.108
Then you're going to shoot a
zip line through that window

00:43:03.108 --> 00:43:05.384
and you're going to slide
on into the hallway.

00:43:05.384 --> 00:43:06.750
You're going to be wearing roller skates

00:43:06.750 --> 00:43:09.260
so you can just skate on
past the armed guards,

00:43:09.260 --> 00:43:10.593
who will be shooting at you.

00:43:10.593 --> 00:43:13.001
And you're going to hope that
they both won't hit your legs,

00:43:13.001 --> 00:43:15.131
and if they are aiming at your torso,

00:43:15.131 --> 00:43:17.540
the suit of medieval armor
will deflect the bullets.

00:43:17.540 --> 00:43:19.316
Then you're going to hope
to gather enough speed

00:43:19.316 --> 00:43:21.058
to burst through the big bank vault

00:43:21.058 --> 00:43:22.218
at the end of the hallway,

00:43:22.218 --> 00:43:23.686
where you're going to collect the money

00:43:23.686 --> 00:43:25.603
in a cartoon money bag.

00:43:29.416 --> 00:43:31.107
- Well, no, I'm just an uncle
who's buying some (beep)

00:43:31.107 --> 00:43:33.459
for Christmas and I think I'll
take my business elsewhere.

00:43:33.459 --> 00:43:35.959
Merry Christmas.
- It's April.

00:43:37.399 --> 00:43:40.878
- [Wife] Neil, Neil.
- Yes, honey.

00:43:40.878 --> 00:43:42.313
- Neil deGrasse Tyson.

00:43:42.313 --> 00:43:44.454
I asked you to take the dog out for a walk

00:43:44.454 --> 00:43:47.204
when I left the house four hours ago.

00:43:47.204 --> 00:43:49.462
Now I come back and this
little (beep) Sputnik

00:43:49.462 --> 00:43:51.962
has done pissed on my drapes.

00:43:51.962 --> 00:43:55.143
Now I've got to get rid of
those things and get new ones.

00:43:55.143 --> 00:43:57.044
Neil, it doesn't make any sense.

00:43:57.044 --> 00:43:59.134
You are an astrophysicist.

00:43:59.134 --> 00:44:04.134
How can you not keep track
of little details like this?

00:44:04.465 --> 00:44:07.882
- Well, actually, it's the little details

00:44:08.796 --> 00:44:12.484
that cannot be kept
track of by definition.

00:44:12.484 --> 00:44:16.134
In 1927, a German university lecturer

00:44:16.134 --> 00:44:17.996
named Warner Heisenberg came

00:44:17.996 --> 00:44:21.484
to a seemingly paradoxical conclusion.

00:44:21.484 --> 00:44:24.243
That the more we know about the position

00:44:24.243 --> 00:44:26.065
of a particle in physical space,

00:44:26.065 --> 00:44:29.779
the less we know about its
momentum and vice versa.

00:44:29.779 --> 00:44:30.862
- Okay, but what--

00:44:30.862 --> 00:44:33.302
- Taken in conjunction with what we know

00:44:33.302 --> 00:44:35.787
about the expansion of the universe,

00:44:35.787 --> 00:44:38.884
this brings us to a
fascinating possibility.

00:44:38.884 --> 00:44:42.323
That maybe what we experience as one point

00:44:42.323 --> 00:44:46.462
in time-space could actually
be a lesion of points,

00:44:46.462 --> 00:44:49.178
which would mean that your curtains

00:44:49.178 --> 00:44:51.952
and little Sputnik's
peepee could be as distant

00:44:51.952 --> 00:44:56.285
from one another as we are
from the furthest galaxy.

00:44:59.012 --> 00:45:01.512
- Okay, well, just, next time.

00:45:09.243 --> 00:45:10.924
- Of course, dear.

00:45:10.924 --> 00:45:12.757
- Neil, there you are.

00:45:15.769 --> 00:45:17.209
What are you doing?

00:45:17.209 --> 00:45:19.226
We have to go to my Aunt Nellie's funeral.

00:45:19.226 --> 00:45:21.797
How are you not ready?

00:45:21.797 --> 00:45:24.427
I've been talking to
you about this all week.

00:45:24.427 --> 00:45:26.018
How am I going to find you in your boxers,

00:45:26.018 --> 00:45:29.267
looking at a science magazine?

00:45:29.267 --> 00:45:32.578
I swear, Neil, sometimes I think you don't

00:45:32.578 --> 00:45:35.411
have any idea of what's important.

00:45:36.517 --> 00:45:40.267
- Well, actually, an
idea of what's important

00:45:41.958 --> 00:45:43.720
is as close as we can ever come

00:45:43.720 --> 00:45:46.797
to any definition of importance.

00:45:46.797 --> 00:45:50.520
Our galaxy is one of
over a hundred billion

00:45:50.520 --> 00:45:52.968
in the observable universe,

00:45:52.968 --> 00:45:56.418
and it's a 100,000 light years across,

00:45:56.418 --> 00:46:01.338
which means it would take light
100,000 years to traverse.

00:46:01.338 --> 00:46:04.621
- Okay, but--
- But that's just space.

00:46:04.621 --> 00:46:08.598
If we were to chart the
history of the universe

00:46:08.598 --> 00:46:10.480
on one calendar year,

00:46:10.480 --> 00:46:13.108
the history of mankind as we know it,

00:46:13.108 --> 00:46:16.458
would just take place in the
final second of that year.

00:46:16.458 --> 00:46:19.875
So whether I'm ready now or in 500 years,

00:46:21.509 --> 00:46:26.197
cosmically speaking, the
distinction is meaningless.

00:46:26.197 --> 00:46:28.530
- Well, okay, I'm going now.

00:46:38.130 --> 00:46:40.130
- Goodbye, honey.
- Neil.

00:46:41.349 --> 00:46:42.517
Who is she?

